        "abstract": "Today's high performance computer systems must have fast, reliable access to memory and I/O devices. Unfortunately, inter-symbol interference, transmission line effects and other noise sources can distort data transfers. Engineers must therefore determine if bus designs have signal integrity <i xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">.</i>e., the bus can transfer data with minimal amplitude or timing distortion. One method of determining signal quality on buses is to conduct a set of data transfers and measure various signal parameters at the receiver end. But the tests must be conducted with stressful test patterns that maximize inter-symbol interference to help identify any potential problems. In this paper we describe how an evolutionary algorithm was used to evolve such test patterns. All test results were obtained intrinsically",
